 ### [Curvature Loss](2911-curvature-loss.md)
-Curvature Loss is a geometric regularization term used in differentiable volume rendering and neural implicit surface optimization to penalize high-frequency oscillations in the predicted scalar field (density or signed distance function) [2911-curvature-loss.md]. It enforces spatial smoothness by computing a discrete approximation of the curvature operator on the model output [2911-curvature-loss.md]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods by operating directly on the scalar field values rather than on derived normal vectors or ray alignments [2911-curvature-loss.md].
+Curvature Loss is a geometric regularization term used in differentiable volume rendering and neural implicit surface optimization to penalize high-frequency oscillations in the predicted scalar field [2911-curvature-loss.md]. It solves the problem of noisy, jagged, or topologically incorrect geometry by enforcing smoothness in the second-order derivatives of the model output [2911-curvature-loss.md]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Normal Consistency and Alignment](13295-normal-consistency-and-alignment.md) by operating on the curvature (second derivative) of the field rather than the alignment of first-order normals, and from [Surface Curvature Regularization](6510-surface-curvature-regularization.md) by serving as the foundational mechanism that those methods build upon [2911-curvature-loss.md].
 
 ### [2D Planar Image Alignment](6845-2d-planar-image-alignment.md)
-2D Planar Image Alignment is a simplified joint optimization problem used to demonstrate the robustness of differentiable rendering pipelines by aligning overlapping 2D image patches with learned content [6845-2d-planar-image-alignment.md]. It solves the problem of reconstructing a ground truth image from transformed patches by simultaneously optimizing the image content representation and the geometric transformation parameters [6845-2d-planar-image-alignment.md]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Curvature Loss](2911-curvature-loss.md) by focusing on geometric consistency via homography warps rather than smoothness priors on the field itself, serving as a 2D analog to the 3D alignment losses in [Geometric and Normal Alignment Losses](4970-geometric-and-normal-alignment-losses.md).
+2D Planar Image Alignment is a simplified joint optimization problem that aligns overlapping 2D image patches with learned content [6845-2d-planar-image-alignment.md]. It reconstructs a ground truth image from transformed patches by simultaneously optimizing the image content representation and the geometric transformation parameters [6845-2d-planar-image-alignment.md]. It differs from other geometric losses by operating in 2D patch space using homography parameters and low-rank tensor decompositions, rather than directly constraining 3D geometry or normals [6845-2d-planar-image-alignment.md].
 
 ### [Material Smoothness Loss](7299-material-smoothness-loss.md)
