 ## Background
-Geometric and normal alignment losses have emerged as critical components in differentiable volume rendering, particularly in Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) and 3D Gaussian Splatting, to address the inherent ambiguity between radiance and geometry. Early works like AligNeRF introduced alignment-aware patch matching to handle spatial misalignment in rendered patches [S1]. Subsequent research, such as the work on architectural surfaces, introduced boundary losses to enforce planar constraints and reduce "floaters" in indoor scenes [S2]. Comparative studies on ray-based losses highlighted the sensitivity of different regularization terms to false geometry, with distortion loss showing high sensitivity [S3]. In the context of vector fields and scientific visualization, domain-specific losses like cosine direction and Jacobian losses were introduced to handle vector data fidelity [S4, unofficial]. More recently, in Gaussian Splatting variants like DP-GES, alignment losses have been adapted to enforce surfel geometry consistency and prevent background leakage [S5, unofficial]. Additionally, feature-based alignment using DINO features has been proposed to overcome photo-consistency violations in joint motion and structure estimation [S6, unofficial].
+The concept of using geometric constraints to regularize neural radiance fields emerged as a response to the "floaters" and noisy geometry artifacts common in sparse-view reconstruction. Early works like NeRF [44-radiance-field-optimization.md] relied primarily on photometric loss, which often led to ambiguous geometry. Subsequent research introduced curvature regularization [2911-curvature-loss.md] and normal consistency losses [13295-normal-consistency-and-alignment.md] to enforce smoothness and plausibility. The alignment-aware patch matching loss [S1] was introduced to handle misalignments in high-frequency details, while boundary losses [S2] specifically targeted architectural surfaces. More recent approaches have extended these principles to material fields [7299-material-smoothness-loss.md] and style transfer contexts [14883-geometry-consistency-loss.md], demonstrating the versatility of geometric regularization in differentiable rendering.
 
